        "problem": "$\\int \\frac{3{x}^{2}-2x+1}{x-1}\\,\\text{d}x=$",
        "worked_solution": "We first need to decompose the integrand into its quotient and remainder parts, which we can do using synthetic division.\n\nWe draw up a synthetic division table:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {x}^{2} & {x}^{1} & {x}^{0} \\\\ 1 & 3 & -2 & 1 \\\\  & \u2193 & 3 & 1 \\\\  & 3 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo from the synthetic division, we get that\n\n$$\n\\frac{3{x}^{2}-2x+1}{x-1}=3x+1+\\frac{2}{x-1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3{x}^{2}-2x+1}{x-1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (3x+1+\\frac{2}{x-1})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\int x\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\text{d}x+2\\int \\frac{1}{x-1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}+x+2\\operatorname{ln}\u2061|x-1|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
